# conversations-casual-chat.kb - Conversations / Casual chat
# Format: concept | intent | answer | keywords | patterns | tl | bis
# 6 entries, one genuinely distinct fact per line.

banter | definition | Banter is teasing conversation between people comfortable enough that the teasing is the whole point. It requires the other person to be able to return it, which is why banter with a stranger reads as hostility while the same words among friends read as affection. The rule that keeps it safe is that it comes from a position of relative safety, and a joke at somebody's expense is not banter however friendly the delivery is. | banter, tease, comfortable, return, affection, joke | what is banter, is banter rude, difference between banter and bullying | banter | binatilyahan
hang out | definition | To hang out is to spend time with someone with no scheduled purpose, and the word is informal and deliberately vague about the activity itself. English has almost no formal word for this, which is a clue about how much of social life is planned for no reason. It is also the most reliable way to build a friendship, more so than talking, because nothing has to be produced and nobody is being assessed. | hang out, spend, time, informal, plan, friendship | what does hang out mean, is hanging out a date, how to hang out with someone | hang out | magkasama
inside joke | definition | An inside joke is one only some people understand, and it marks a group by exclusion as much as by humour. That is what makes it funny within the group and baffling to anybody outside it, and it is why telling an inside joke to an outsider kills it completely. The mechanism underneath is shared knowledge that costs nothing to acquire and cannot be explained without destroying the point of it. | inside joke, group, humour, outsider, shared, explain | what is an inside joke, why are inside jokes funny, how to explain an inside joke | inside joke | sa loob ng grupo
awkward | definition | Awkward describes a social situation where the participants are unsure how to act and each is waiting for the other to go first. It is not the same as uncomfortable, which is a physical or emotional state, since a situation can be awkward and nobody mind it at all. The usual causes are a gap in shared knowledge, an unclear role, or simply being new to a group, and the reliable cure is somebody naming it, which is why this is awkward actually works. | awkward, situation, unsure, act, uncomfortable, name | what does awkward mean, is an awkward silence normal, how to fix an awkward moment | awkward | kahiya
downtime | definition | Downtime is a period when nothing productive is scheduled, and the word is neutral about whether that is a loss. In operations it names idle stretches that are actively reduced, while in ordinary conversation it names the gap between events, which is often the part people later remember. The word also covers waiting on a machine, and there it carries a mild irritation that it does not carry for a holiday. | downtime, idle, scheduled, gap, waiting, remember | what is downtime, is downtime good, difference between downtime and break | downtime | downtime
ramble | definition | To ramble is to talk at length without a clear thread, and the word is usually critical in a way that describing the same behaviour as exploring is not. What separates rambling from being detailed is whether the speaker knows where they are going, since detail follows a path and rambling does not. In a conversation it is also recoverable, because asking what the point was usually produces one, and that is why the interruption works better than the silence. | ramble, length, thread, detail, explore, interrupt | what does ramble mean, is rambling bad, how to stop rambling | ramble | umiikot
